<article class="env-lemma" id="0AV1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AV1">Lemma <span data-tag="0AV1">15.24.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian domain. Let $M$ be a finite $R$-module. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$M$ is reflexive, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$M_\mathfrak p$ is a reflexive $R_\mathfrak p$-module for all primes $\mathfrak p \subset R$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$M_\mathfrak m$ is a reflexive $R_\mathfrak m$-module for all maximal ideals $\mathfrak m$ of $R$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The localization of $j : M \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(M, R), R)$ at a prime $\mathfrak p$ is the corresponding map for the module $M_\mathfrak p$ over the Noetherian local domain $R_\mathfrak p$. See Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0583" data-tag="0583">10.10.2</a>. Thus the lemma holds by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00HN" data-tag="00HN">10.23.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>